<!--
  Everything THIS application serves that is meant to be found. Two pages, and the rest of
  the file is why nothing else is here.

  THE PROTOTYPES. They live under /feature-demo/ — agent, strategy-modeler, config,
  pairs-monitor, studio, ui-mocks — they render invented figures for products that do not
  exist yet, and every one of them answers with X-Robots-Tag: noindex (Program.cs argues
  why noindex and not a robots Disallow). Listing a noindex URL asks a crawler to index
  something the page then refuses, which Search Console records as an error against the
  sitemap rather than a note against the page.

  Their old top-level addresses — /agent, /config, /strategy-modeler, /pairs-monitor —
  still answer, with a 302 to the new one. Neither end belongs here: a redirect is not a
  page, and the page it redirects to is noindex.

  /STUDIO IS NOT THIS APPLICATION'S PAGE AT ALL, and that is the change this file exists to
  record. It used to be one of the prototypes above. It now belongs to Studio — a separate
  application, its own container behind the same proxy, serving the public comparison site
  under UsePathBase("/studio"). This application does not answer there, so it must not
  describe what does. Studio publishes its own sitemap at /studio/sitemap.xml, generated from
  the same query that renders its pages, and robots.txt names it on a line of its own.

  Two alternatives were rejected, both in plans/sitemaps.md at length. Making this file a
  sitemap index over Studio's: a file is either <urlset> or <sitemapindex>, never both, so
  the two pages below would have had to move into a second file and this URL would change
  meaning under a crawler that already has it. Listing Studio's pages here: it hands one
  application the job of keeping another application's inventory current, and this one
  holds the read-write database credential that would make doing it possible.

  NO changefreq AND NO priority, and they were here until this edit. Google, on the page
  that defines the format it accepts: "Google ignores <priority> and <changefreq> values"
  (developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/sitemaps/build-sitemap). No other
  consumer of this file was ever named. What they were, then, was two hand-maintained
  fields that had to be kept plausible for nobody — and the generated sitemap Studio is
  about to serve on this same host omits them for the same reason. Two sitemaps on one host
  should not disagree about what a sitemap is for.

  lastmod is edited by hand, which is affordable at two pages and is exactly what it stops
  being at Studio's scale — that file computes it instead, from a column, and the argument
  for which column is the long half of plans/sitemaps.md.

  The front page moves to 2026-09-06: d6ccc66 repointed all five Features links from the
  top-level prototype addresses to /feature-demo/. Google's own line for what earns a new
  lastmod names link changes beside main content, and these are the links a reader clicks.
  /zurnalas stays at 2026-09-01 — nothing has touched ui-mocks/zurnalas.html since 88e60e7
  moved the page into this application, and a date that moves without the page moving is
  the one thing that makes a crawler stop believing the field.
-->
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <url>
    <loc>https://cryptosmithx.blynai.eu/</loc>
    <lastmod>2026-09-06</lastmod>
  </url>
  <url>
    <loc>https://cryptosmithx.blynai.eu/zurnalas</loc>
    <lastmod>2026-09-01</lastmod>
  </url>
</urlset>
